# Comments

The best comment is no comment — code that is clear enough that none is needed. Every comment that has to be written is a failure to express intent in code. Comments compensate for code, not complement it.

## CMT-01 — Explain why, not what

Code tells you how. Comments tell you why. Write comments that explain business decisions, architectural constraints, and non-obvious trade-offs — not what the code does.

```
// bad — narrates the code; adds no value
// increment the counter
count++

// good — explains a non-obvious constraint
// start at 1, not 0: question numbers are 1-indexed in exported PDFs
count = 1
```

## CMT-02 — Use comments as explanation of intent

A comment is justified when it explains the reason behind a decision that cannot be encoded in the code itself — why an algorithm was chosen, why a workaround exists, or what constraint the code satisfies.

```
// bad — states the obvious
// returns the user
getUser(id)

// good — explains the why
// fetch from replica to avoid overloading the primary during bulk exports
getUser(id, source: replica)
```

## CMT-03 — Use comments as warning of consequences

Warn future maintainers when a piece of code has surprising implications — performance cost, ordering dependency, required external state, or a known fragility.

```
// warning — do not call this inside a transaction; it opens a nested connection
// that deadlocks the parent transaction on Postgres
rebuildSearchIndex()
```

## CMT-04 — Do not be redundant

Do not write comments that restate what the name or signature already says. Redundant comments become stale and mislead more than silence would.

```
// bad
// sets the user's name
user.name = name

// bad — the signature already says this
// @param id — the user id
// @returns the user
getUser(id): User
```

## CMT-05 — Do not add obvious noise

Do not add comments that state something universally obvious — constructors, getters, and simple assignments need no explanation.

```
// bad
// constructor
User(name, email):
    // set name field
    self.name = name
    // set email field
    self.email = email
```

## CMT-06 — Do not use closing-brace comments

Do not mark the end of a block with a comment. Long enough to need a marker is long enough to extract into a named function.

```
// bad
for item in items:
    if item.active:
        process(item)
    // end if active
// end for items

// good — extract if the block is complex enough to need a label
processActiveItems(items)
```

## CMT-07 — Never comment out code — remove it

Commented-out code is noise. It accumulates, loses context, and misleads readers about what is current. Delete it. Version control preserves it if it is ever needed again.

```
// bad
// oldImplementation(x)
// legacyFallback(x)
newImplementation(x)

// good
newImplementation(x)
```

## CMT-08 — Do not generate documentation unless requested

Do not create, update, or add documentation files unless explicitly asked. Prohibited unless requested:

- README updates
- API docs or inline doc-comment blocks added speculatively
- Architecture guides or markdown files
- Changelogs or release notes

When asked to implement code, deliver implementation only.
